        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Matters When Buying a Dishwasher
          </h2>
          <p>
            Every dishwasher on a showroom floor claims to be quiet, efficient, and powerful. In practice, five specs separate a dishwasher you will love from one you will regret within a year: noise rating, drying technology, third rack presence, place-setting capacity, and soil sensor accuracy. Here is what each one actually means once the machine is installed in your kitchen.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Noise Rating (Decibels) — The Spec Buyers Get Wrong Most Often</h3>
          <p>
            Noise rating is, in our experience, the single most underrated spec on a dishwasher and the one buyers most often regret ignoring. Dishwasher noise is measured in decibels (dB), and the scale is logarithmic — a 10 dB difference sounds roughly twice as loud to the human ear, not just marginally louder. A machine rated 51 dB is not a little louder than one rated 41 dB; it is dramatically more intrusive, especially in the open-concept kitchens that dominate modern home layouts, where the dishwasher runs a few feet from the dining table or living room. Buyers shopping primarily on price frequently end up with a machine in the high-40s to low-50s dB range and only discover how loud that actually is after it interrupts the first dinner party or phone call. If your kitchen is closed off from your main living space, a noisier machine is a reasonable trade-off for savings. If it is not, treat the dB rating as a top-three priority, not an afterthought.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drying Technology: Condensation vs. Fan-Assisted vs. Heated</h3>
          <p>
            Drying is where most dishwashers quietly disappoint their owners, particularly with plastic items. Basic condensation drying relies on the temperature difference between the hot rinse water and the cooler stainless tub to passively condense moisture off the dishes — it works reasonably well on glass and ceramic, which retain heat, but plastic containers stay cool and simply do not dry through condensation alone, which is why you regularly open a dishwasher to find water pooled in the bottom of a plastic container. Fan-assisted drying actively circulates air through the tub during the dry cycle, meaningfully improving plastic drying results, as we saw with the GE Profile's Dry Boost system in our testing. Heated drying uses a heating element to actively evaporate moisture and typically produces the best all-around results, though it uses more energy per cycle. The newest category, mineral-assisted drying like Bosch's CrystalDry, uses a zeolite mineral that releases heat as it absorbs moisture, and in our testing it produced the best plastic-drying results of any technology we evaluated.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Third Rack: Worth It or Marketing?</h3>
          <p>
            A dishwasher with a third rack is genuinely useful, but the execution varies enormously between brands, and this is a spec where reading the fine print matters. Some third racks are little more than a shallow plastic tray with barely enough clearance for a teaspoon. Others, like the GE Profile in our test group, have real depth and can hold a full-length butter knife or spatula flat. The functional benefit of a good third rack is not just extra capacity — it is removing long, awkwardly shaped utensils from the lower rack, where they commonly block the spray arm's rotation and cause the entire load to come out dirtier. If you regularly wash cooking utensils, tongs, or spatulas, prioritize a dishwasher with a genuinely deep third rack over one that includes the feature purely as a checkbox.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Place Settings Capacity</h3>
          <p>
            Manufacturers rate capacity in place settings, a standardized measurement representing one full meal's worth of dishes per person, including a dinner plate, salad plate, bowl, glass, and a set of flatware. Standard dishwashers typically run 12 to 14 place settings; larger capacity models push toward 16. The number matters less in isolation than in context of your household — a family of four running one load a day is well served by a 12-14 place-setting machine, while households that entertain regularly or run large family dinners benefit meaningfully from the jump to 16 place settings, since it often means the difference between one load and two after a holiday meal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soil Sensor Accuracy</h3>
          <p>
            Nearly every mid-range and premium dishwasher now advertises a soil sensor, but sensor quality varies more than manufacturers like to admit. A soil sensor measures water turbidity — essentially how cloudy the wash water becomes — partway through the cycle, and uses that reading to decide whether to extend wash time, add another rinse, or increase water temperature. A well-calibrated sensor, like the one in the KitchenAid ProWash cycle we tested, can tell the difference between a lightly soiled load of coffee mugs and a pan with baked-on lasagna, and adjusts accordingly without you ever selecting a cycle manually. A poorly calibrated sensor either runs every load on the longest cycle regardless of actual soil level, wasting water and time, or under-detects heavy soil and leaves food behind. In our testing, sensor accuracy correlated more closely with overall satisfaction than almost any other single spec, including price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Energy Efficiency and Running Costs</h3>
          <p>
            A dishwasher's sticker price is only part of what it costs you over its lifespan. Energy Star-certified models use meaningfully less water and electricity per cycle than non-certified machines, and over eight to twelve years of daily use that difference adds up to a real dollar figure on your utility bill, not just an environmental talking point. In our testing, the most efficient machines used roughly 3 gallons of water per cycle, while the least efficient used closer to 6, which compounds fast across a household running one load a day. Longer cycle times, like the KitchenAid's 2.5-hour Normal cycle, are frequently the trade-off for that efficiency — the machine heats water more gradually and washes at a slightly lower temperature for longer, rather than blasting hot water for a shorter period, which uses more energy per cycle even though it finishes faster. If you run your dishwasher daily and plan to keep it for a decade, paying slightly more upfront for an Energy Star-rated model with a strong soil sensor is usually the better long-term financial decision, since it uses only as much water and energy as a given load actually requires instead of running every cycle at maximum resource use.
          </p>

