        <!-- SECTION 4: Hard Water / Spotty Dishes -->
        <div id="hard-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Your Dishes Might Still Be Spotty
          </h2>
          <p>
            Here's something we ran into repeatedly during testing that has nothing to do with which dishwasher you buy: if your glasses come out cloudy, your flatware has a chalky film, and your detergent never seems to fully dissolve or rinse clean no matter which brand or pod you switch to, the problem may not be the dishwasher at all. It's frequently a water hardness issue, and even the best dishwasher on this list cannot fully compensate for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Hard water contains high concentrations of dissolved calcium and magnesium. When that water heats up during a wash cycle and then evaporates during drying, it leaves behind mineral deposits on glassware, flatware, and the interior of the dishwasher itself — the cloudy film on glasses, the white chalky residue on black dishes, and the gritty buildup on the heating element and spray arm nozzles over time. Hard water also directly interferes with detergent chemistry: dissolved minerals bind with detergent surfactants before they ever reach your dishes, meaning a portion of every detergent dose is neutralized by the water itself rather than doing the job of lifting food residue. This is why households in hard-water regions often report needing more detergent, more rinse aid, and still getting worse results than households with naturally soft water using less of everything.
          </p>
          <p>
            Rinse aid helps mask the symptom — it changes the surface tension of water so it sheets off glassware instead of forming droplets that dry into spots — but it does not address the underlying mineral content of your water supply. If you've tried multiple detergents, run the hottest wash cycle available, used rinse aid consistently, and your dishes are still coming out spotty or filmed, hard water is the most likely root cause, and no dishwasher model swap will fully fix it. The permanent fix is a whole-home water softener, which removes calcium and magnesium from your water supply before it ever reaches your dishwasher, water heater, or fixtures. We cover exactly how these systems work, what to look for, and our top-tested picks in our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold no-underline">Best Water Softener System guide</a> — it's the natural next read if spotty dishes, scale buildup, or dull-feeling water are recurring problems in your home, not just at the dishwasher.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-blue-700 mb-2">Quick Hard Water Check</p>
            <p class="text-blue-900 text-sm">If soap struggles to lather in your sink, you notice white scale buildup around faucets and showerheads, or your dishwasher's heating element has visible crusty deposits after a year of use, your water is very likely hard. A water hardness test kit or a call to your local water utility can confirm it in minutes, and our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/" class="text-blue-700 font-bold no-underline">water softener guide</a> walks through the fix in full.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

